WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:05.809
There’s an ancient Arabic legend about a
shapeshifter that can take the form of any

00:00:05.809 --> 00:00:06.809
animal.

00:00:06.809 --> 00:00:12.030
They can also appear as a living column of
smoke or dust, or a giant human— and have

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:14.330
also been known to live trapped in a bottle.

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:20.029
Jinn are genies - ancient creatures believed
to occupy the space between celestial and

00:00:20.029 --> 00:00:21.810
terrestrial worlds.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:27.500
Not quite monsters, not quite humans, and
not quite celestial beings, the jinn are something

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:28.500
different.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.999
But for many people, the genie is just some
guy in a lamp who can give you three wishes.

00:00:31.999 --> 00:00:36.460
A way for you to get what you want without
having to work for it.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:41.399
Not only is that not even close to the original
versions of the jinn, it dismisses the immense

00:00:41.399 --> 00:00:43.800
power these beings possess.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.629
The jinn are a species all their own, more
powerful than humans, and more meaningful

00:00:48.629 --> 00:00:52.399
than pop culture would have us believe.

00:00:52.399 --> 00:00:56.719
[Monstrum intro.]

00:00:56.719 --> 00:01:02.420
Crafted from prehistoric fires, long before
humans, Jinn are neither wholly earthly nor

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:03.980
wholly spiritual.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:08.770
They require food to live, are subject to
lust and other human passions, and can die,

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:11.790
although they live naturally for thousands
of years.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:15.070
Jinn can see humans and interfere in their
lives.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:19.280
Humans can only see a jinni if it takes a
form that allows itself to be seen, which

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.360
is rare.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:25.010
They can move through walls and travel great
distances quickly, shapeshifting into all

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:28.050
kinds of animals and can appear as smoke.

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:32.610
Sometimes the appearance of a jinni is preceded
by an earthquake or a storm.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:38.060
In ancient Arabic cultures, jinn were worshipped
for their capacity to make legendary weapons

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.750
and supposed ability to encourage fertile
crops.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:45.191
These pre-Islamic accounts of jinn also claimed
a jinni could take on a human form and and

00:01:45.191 --> 00:01:46.680
have children with them.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:51.120
These offspring were often seers who could
communicate with their jinn relatives as well

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:52.740
as with the divine.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:57.070
These half-jinn children were advisors and
consultants of religious matters.

00:01:57.070 --> 00:02:02.460
They helped find lost animals, gave and removed
curses, and settled personal disputes.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:06.660
Islamic narratives heavily influenced the
perception of jinn and their characteristics.

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:13.280
The Qur’an explicitly mentions there are
three types of spiritual beings: angels, demons,

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:14.560
and jinn.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:19.450
That text also states jinn were created before
humans and are intelligent beings with free

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:23.440
will, formed from “smokeless fire” and
“scorching winds.”

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:28.520
In Islamic tradition, people made from clay
occupy the material realm of Earth, while

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:30.960
angels occupy the celestial realm of light.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:34.490
It’s said that jinn, being made from fire,
reside in a realm between the material and

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:37.530
celestial—called the intermediate realm.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:41.450
A particular story in the Qur’an tells of
an encounter between the prophet Muhammad

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:43.140
and a crowd of jinn.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:48.040
This tale describes the jinn as “men, tall
as lances, completely wrapped in their mantles

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:51.810
from their feet up” who seem to be composed
of “blackness.”

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:58.050
Interestingly, the 55th chapter of the Qur’an
is written in duel form for both humans and

00:02:58.050 --> 00:02:59.050
jinn.

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:03.400
It describes Jinn as practitioners of religion,
including Islam, who are believed to have

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:06.300
the same religious responsibilities as humans.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:10.680
Medieval Muslim scholars believed the jinn
were susceptible to fiery temperaments because

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:12.670
they were composed of fire.

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:14.770
So jinn could either be good or evil.

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:19.500
The evil jinn were said to enter into the
bodies of humans, causing madness, injury,

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:23.630
or death—an idea that pre-Islamic communities
shared as well.

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:27.630
Human and jinn interactions weren’t always
negative—stories of love affairs between

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:31.069
jinn and humans were popular in the Medieval
period.

00:03:31.069 --> 00:03:35.330
One of the most famous interactions between
jinn and humans occur in one of the world’s

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:39.720
most popular texts— The Thousand and One
Nights.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:44.470
Scholars argue the famous collection of stories
takes its inspiration from a number of sources

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:49.010
including Indo-Persian and Greek traditions
as well as Arabic-Islamic ones.

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:54.060
One thing for certain is that although a 9th-century
fragment and a 14th -century manuscript of

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:59.520
The Thousand and One Nights have been found,
there still no conclusive source.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.790
Since jinn figure heavily in various stories,
we can assume that some aspects of these creatures

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:07.540
exist across many cultural histories.

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:12.250
With each translation and variation of the
text, older and newer tales were added and

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:17.549
subtracted regularly, but the jinn appear
in all known editions.

00:04:17.549 --> 00:04:21.199
One significant story in the collection is
“The Story of the Fisherman.”

00:04:21.199 --> 00:04:25.879
In it, an old man slogs through a hard day
of work, with nothing to show for it.

00:04:25.879 --> 00:04:31.099
After casting his net many times, he draws
up a bottle of brass with a heavy lead stopper

00:04:31.099 --> 00:04:33.300
stamped with the seal of a dead sultan.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:38.729
When he opens the bottle, a plume of smoke
rises up and condenses into the form of a

00:04:38.729 --> 00:04:44.150
massive ‘Efreet, an evil jinni, so big his
head is inside the clouds.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:48.270
At first the jinni, having been trapped in
the bottle for 1,800 years, wants to kill

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:49.460
the fisherman.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:51.600
But the old fisherman is clever.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:55.840
He challenges the jinni to show him how it
was possible for such a magnificent, powerful

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:58.680
being to fit in such a small vessel.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:03.440
Apparently very vain, the jinni turns back
into smoke and enters the bottle little by

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:08.300
little until the fisherman can seal it up,
once again trapping the Jinni.

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:13.450
After some negotiation the two come to a compromise—if
the fisherman releases him the jinni will

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:17.949
not only let the fisherman live, but will
grant him one service.

00:05:17.949 --> 00:05:22.449
Once freed, the jinni leads the man to a hidden
lake in the desert where he is able to capture

00:05:22.449 --> 00:05:25.009
the most exquisitely colored fish.

00:05:25.009 --> 00:05:28.509
The rare fish make the fisherman a very prosperous
man.

00:05:28.509 --> 00:05:32.069
Another important tale is “The Story of
the Merchant and the Jinnee.”

00:05:32.069 --> 00:05:36.370
This version tells the story of a merchant
who rests under a tree, eating dates, when

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:38.400
he carelessly tosses a pit aside.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:44.509
An angry jinni appears, sword drawn, claiming
the pit had struck and killed his son (remember,

00:05:44.509 --> 00:05:48.360
jinni cannot be seen by humans in their natural
form).

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:52.240
Before he slays the man, he agrees to give
the merchant a year to get his affairs in

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:53.240
order.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:57.530
A year passes, and the merchant returns to
the same spot under the tree and awaits his

00:05:57.530 --> 00:05:58.530
death.

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:02.759
While sitting there three sheiks stumble upon
him one by one, each deciding to wait with

00:06:02.759 --> 00:06:03.759
the doomed man.

00:06:03.759 --> 00:06:07.729
When the jinni finally appears the sheiks
strike a bargain—they will each tell the

00:06:07.729 --> 00:06:09.120
entity a story.

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:13.029
If one of the stories pleases the jinni, he
will relinquish one third of his claim on

00:06:13.029 --> 00:06:14.389
the merchant’s life.

00:06:14.389 --> 00:06:19.409
Luckily, each story impresses the jinni and
the merchant survives.

00:06:19.409 --> 00:06:23.889
In the early 18th century, Antoine Galland,
a French archeologist and scholar was the

00:06:23.889 --> 00:06:28.770
first person to translate the arabic collection
into a European language.

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:33.599
He procureda 3 or 4 volume manuscript from
the Eastern Mediterranean region of Levant,

00:06:33.599 --> 00:06:36.129
dated between the 14th and 15th century.

00:06:36.129 --> 00:06:42.199
The translation of these volumes became a
12-volume series published between 1704-1717

00:06:42.199 --> 00:06:46.289
under the title The Thousand and One Nights,
Arab stories translated into French [Les mille

00:06:46.289 --> 00:06:47.289
et une nuits, contes arabes traduitsen francais].

00:06:47.289 --> 00:06:51.020
Despite containing only 280 nights, the series
was a hit.

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:56.110
For nearly a century, this version became
the main text for subsequent translations

00:06:56.110 --> 00:07:02.349
and ironically, in 1814 Galland’s French
text was translated back to Arabic, while

00:07:02.349 --> 00:07:05.379
the original Arabic source faded into obscurity.

00:07:05.379 --> 00:07:09.729
Besides becoming the most commonly translated
version for many years, Galland’s text gave

00:07:09.729 --> 00:07:13.999
the world something else—the stories of
Aladdin and Ali Baba.

00:07:13.999 --> 00:07:18.240
He added these to the collection from oral
versions reportedly told to him by a monk

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:19.680
from Syria.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:22.360
Both stories have no direct Arabic source.

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:26.539
The title Arabian Nights’ Entertainments
was first given to The Thousand and One Nights

00:07:26.539 --> 00:07:32.490
collection by an anonymous Grub street English
translator of Galland’s version in 1706.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:36.409
Arguably, part of the reason all of the versions
of The Thousand and One Nights became instant

00:07:36.409 --> 00:07:42.490
sensations is because Europeans hoped to gain
some insight into the lands they had colonized.

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:46.669
Despite being sensationalized and exaggerated
to say the least, the stories were seen as

00:07:46.669 --> 00:07:48.750
a window into foreign cultures.

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:55.689
In 1840, Edward William Lane, published the
first direct Arabic to English edition based

00:07:55.689 --> 00:07:58.059
on an Egyptian version of the stories.

00:07:58.059 --> 00:08:02.699
At Lane’s request, this Arabic version was
corrected and annotated by Sheikh Muhammad

00:08:02.699 --> 00:08:07.930
al-Tantawi who helped translate the challenging
Middle Arabic words for more easy conversion

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:08.930
to English.

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:14.879
Lane’s version also included detailed research
notes on Arabic culture as well as illustrations.

00:08:14.879 --> 00:08:18.810
In some Islamic traditions today, the jinn
are said to still roam the Earth, hidden from

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:19.929
humans.

00:08:19.929 --> 00:08:24.149
And among some people, it is believed that
jinn are the cause of mental illness.

00:08:24.149 --> 00:08:29.460
Hallucinations, epilepsy, and other conditions
are said to be the work of jinn.

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:33.750
When one considers that jinn are part of Muslim
religious tradition it’s understadable that

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:35.409
people would think this.

00:08:35.409 --> 00:08:40.940
Much in the same way other religions conceptualize
demonic possession, a jinni occupying a human’s

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:44.760
body is one explanation for seemingly uncontrollable
behavior.

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:48.459
The word “genie” is an Anglicanized version
of the French word [Génies] first used by

00:08:48.459 --> 00:08:54.170
Galland in 1706, a term itself borrowed from
the Latin word “genius” referencing a

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:56.319
supernatural guardian spirit.

00:08:56.319 --> 00:09:00.450
The first reference of the genie granting
three wishes also appeared in Galland’s

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:03.790
text, in his rendition of “The Story of
the Fisherman”.

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:08.180
In that story, the jinni explains that he
initially thought about granting the person

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:12.180
who freed him from the bottle “three demands”
every day.

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:16.350
Since then, the three demands or wishes have
become a common jinn trope, even appearing

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:20.360
the first time a jinni appears in film.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:29.690
The iconic Genie portrayed by Robin Williams
in Disney’s 1992 Aladdin is a hybrid of

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:33.270
all these iterations.

00:09:33.270 --> 00:09:40.620
However, this time the jinni retains some
control over the three wishes he must grant,

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:48.190
giving Aladdin stipulations, including that
he cannot wish for more wishes.

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:53.930
From spiritual being to comical cartoon—that’s
quite a journey.

00:09:53.930 --> 00:09:58.399
Jinn have shown up in traditions and stories
since the pre-Islamic Arabic world, but it

00:09:58.399 --> 00:10:03.029
wasn’t until a few stories were translated
in the 18th century, that these beings were

00:10:03.029 --> 00:10:04.029
popularized.

00:10:04.029 --> 00:10:08.060
They’re more than just some blue guy trapped
in a lamp who grants wishes.

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:13.310
They’re powerful, intellectual entities
whose stories are woven into ancient Islamic

00:10:13.310 --> 00:10:19.000
culture—and, yes, also capable of changing
the direction of your life in an instant.

